<LabLayout title="CardioRotovator model & assumptions" description="Equations, scope and numerical checks for the finite-mass eccentric single-arm tether experiment.">
<main class="guide" id="lab-content"><header class="guide-header"><p class="micro">C1p-0.1.0 / MODEL & ASSUMPTIONS</p><h1>A long arm needs the right phase.</h1><p>Compare an empty eccentric rotor with the same facility immediately after an ideal matched pickup.</p><a class="button-link primary" href="/lab/cardio/">Open CardioRotovator →</a></header>
<section class="guide-section"><h2>The reference concept</h2><p>HASTOL describes a single tapered arm phased inward at apogee and outward at perigee, with two rotations per orbit. Its analysis identifies pickup-induced orbital change and the need for active resynchronization as major difficulties. See <a href="https://www.niac.usra.edu/files/studies/final_report/355Bogar.pdf#page=21">HASTOL Phase I, pp. 17–19, Figure 13</a>.</p><p>This is a separate numerical experiment, not a reproduction of the report’s atmospheric launch system. The default geometry remains above the atmosphere and deliberately leaves synchronization uncontrolled.</p></section>
<section class="guide-section"><h2>What is calculated</h2><p>A finite station, linearly tapered rigid arm and 2 t grapple move in planar spherical-Earth gravity. The station-to-tip coordinate is s; the body’s mass-weighted center is c. For center-of-mass position R, velocity V, angle θ and spin ω, each material point has position R + (s − c)e and velocity V + (s − c)ωe⊥.</p><p>Sum gravitational force at every mass element to accelerate the center of mass. Sum torques about that center to obtain angular acceleration. No prescribed station orbit, constant-spin motor or phase-lock controller drives the solid replay paths. The optional dashed curve instead uses a Kepler center-of-mass orbit and constant spin, solely as a geometric reference.</p><p>The initial center-of-mass apogee and perigee define the osculating ellipse and nominal period T. Initial angular speed is the selected ratio × 2π/T; zero phase offset points the working arm inward at apogee. This initial ratio is not maintained. Unwrapped spin drift measures θ − (θ₀ + ω₀t), so it includes any lost or gained full turns.</p></section>
<section class="guide-section"><h2>Ideal pickup and two coasts</h2><p>The incoming payload starts at exactly the tip position and velocity. Adding it changes the combined center of mass, its translational velocity and inertia. The state is reframed so every existing material point retains its position and velocity. Combined energy and angular momentum include the arriving payload and close across the attachment.</p><p>The empty and loaded facilities then coast independently for at most one original nominal orbit. The payload remains attached; there is no release. The table’s initial perigee is the point-orbit estimate for each center-of-mass state, not a cable-clearance guarantee. A completed coast means that this sampled run did not reach a stop limit; it does not establish a repeatable pickup.</p></section>
<section class="guide-section"><h2>Mass, load and clearance</h2><p>The default uses an 800 km arm, a 5,000 t station, 10 t incoming payload, 500 mm² tip cable area and a 3:1 station-to-tip area ratio. Initial COM altitudes are 1,200 km perigee and 1,800 km apogee. These are experiment settings. Zylon HM density and strength use the existing Lab material profile, with safety factor two; the linear taper is not optimized.</p><p>Forty-eight cable cells use two-point Gauss quadrature. This integrates the linear taper’s mass, first moment and rotational inertia exactly; gravity and potential are approximated at the same nodes. Axial cut forces sum the outboard inertial-minus-gravitational forces. Transverse reaction is reported separately because a rigid line can require forces a flexible cable cannot provide.</p><p>Clearance uses the closest point on the entire straight arm segment. Each coast stops below 120 km, above the axial allowable or when a cut requires more than 100 N of compression. Classical RK4 steps are at most five seconds and 0.01 rad of rotation; a detected limit is bracketed within the step. Checks are numerical samples, not a certified continuous clearance bound. Energy/angular-momentum drift, capture closure and step/quadrature convergence tests are available in the report and source.</p></section>
<section class="guide-section"><h2>What remains outside the model</h2><p>No atmospheric ascent, incoming guidance, grapple dynamics, capture shock, flexible cable, bending qualification, reeling, motor control, reboost, orbit perturbations or repeatable traffic service is solved. An ideal matched pickup is supplied, not discovered. The rigid-body result is useful for comparing phase and orbital sensitivity within those assumptions.</p><p>CardioRotovator designs have their own browser save key and versioned JSON. They do not overwrite Earth, Moon, Phobos, T4 or Expeditions saves. This experiment cannot be commissioned into Expeditions; the Earth bridge still requires its separate two-delivery model.</p></section>
